  "prediction": "**Department of Defense's Efforts to Prevent and Respond to Unwanted Sexual Behaviors**\n\nThe Department of Defense (DOD) has defined various types of unwanted sexual behaviors, including sexual assault, sexual harassment, and domestic violence. The report highlights the importance of addressing these behaviors to prevent and respond to instances of sexual assault and harassment in the military.\n\n**Current Policies and Procedures**\n\nDOD has policies and procedures in place to prevent and respond to unwanted sexual behaviors, including the development of a comprehensive sexual harassment policy that includes prevention training, mechanisms for reporting incidents, and mechanisms for responding to and resolving instances of sexual harassment. However, the report notes that some policies do not include key elements, such as anonymous reporting of sexual harassment and principles in the Centers for Disease Control's (CDC) framework for preventing sexual violence.\n\n**CDC Framework for Preventing Sexual Violence**\n\nThe CDC framework for preventing sexual violence includes six principles: risk factors, protective factors, primary strategies for prevention, secondary strategies for prevention, tertiary strategies for prevention, and risk domains. The report notes that DOD's policies do not consistently include all of these principles, which may be limiting the effectiveness of prevention efforts.\n\n**Data Collection and Reporting**\n\nThe report highlights inconsistencies in data collection and reporting on unwanted sexual behaviors, including sexual assault and harassment. DOD has developed reliable data systems for collecting and reporting data on some of these behaviors, but inconsistencies in data elements and definitions may be creating difficulties in developing department-wide reports.\n\n**Collaborative Efforts**\n\nDOD has established various collaborative efforts to address unwanted sexual behaviors, including the Sexual Assault Prevention and Response Integrated Product Team, the Prevention Collaboration Forum, and the Victim Assistance Leadership Council. However, the report notes that these efforts may not be fully effective in addressing the complex issues surrounding unwanted sexual behaviors.\n\n**Recommendations**\n\nThe report makes four recommendations to DOD:\n\n1. Fully include the principles in the CDC framework for preventing sexual violence in the new policy for sexual harassment.\n2. Include mechanisms for anonymous reporting of incidents in the new policy for sexual harassment.\n3. Develop standard data elements and definitions for maintaining and reporting information on sexual harassment incidents at the military service level.\n4. Incorporate all the elements that are key for establishing a long-term, results-oriented strategic planning framework in the continuum of harm prevention strategy.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the importance of addressing unwanted sexual behaviors in the military and recommends that DOD take steps to improve its policies, procedures, and data collection efforts to prevent and respond to these behaviors.",
